WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
This protein sits in the right place, at the right time.

2
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.880
Does that tell you what it does?

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.320
Here are two possible answers, each true in its own cell.

4
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:18.313
Call the cells twins: identical, except each has a different model built in.

5
00:00:19.513 --> 00:00:21.973
Every experiment runs on both at once.

6
00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:27.033
Here is the rule: a result can choose only where the twins disagree.

7
00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:30.727
First question: where does the protein live?

8
00:00:32.347 --> 00:00:34.807
Model one, in the teal twin: in lysosomes.

9
00:00:36.207 --> 00:00:39.007
Model two, in the orange twin: in mitochondria.

10
00:00:40.247 --> 00:00:45.167
Break both cells open and spin the pieces, at rising speeds, into five tubes.

11
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:52.667
Test one. Is the protein enriched in tube L, more concentrated than in the starting

12
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:55.447
mix? In the teal twin, yes.

13
00:00:56.767 --> 00:01:01.267
In the orange twin, also yes: tube L holds plenty of mitochondria.

14
00:01:02.507 --> 00:01:03.767
This test cannot choose.

15
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.830
Test two reads all five tubes.

16
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.370
Each bar is the protein's share in one tube.

17
00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.470
Two faint tracks show where the lysosome marker and the mitochondrial marker went.

18
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.770
If the protein is lysosomal, which track should it trace?

19
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.940
Here the twins disagree.

20
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.480
In the lesson's made-up data, the protein follows the lysosome track across all five

21
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.400
tubes. A whole profile is stronger evidence than one peak, though still not proof.

22
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:45.313
Tube L leans toward lysosomes, but leaning is not purity.

23
00:01:46.713 --> 00:01:48.513
So what is tube L mostly made of?

24
00:01:49.693 --> 00:01:50.693
Hold that question.

25
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:55.797
Second question: in what order does a protein visit the compartments?

26
00:01:57.037 --> 00:01:58.397
Tag one batch and watch.

27
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:03.637
In the teal twin, carriers ferry it between fixed Golgi compartments.

28
00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:08.397
In the orange twin, each Golgi compartment itself matures.

29
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:14.657
In both twins, the gold label lights the E R, then the Golgi, then the granules.

30
00:02:15.877 --> 00:02:17.457
The order alone cannot choose.

31
00:02:18.817 --> 00:02:19.817
Other tests can.

32
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.830
Third question: does the protein cause what you see?

33
00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.230
A drug stops these cancer cells growing.

34
00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:33.050
It was built to block one kinase, an enzyme that adds phosphate to proteins.

35
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.890
The curve shows growth falling as the dose rises.

36
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.260
Now take the kinase out of both twins.

37
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.680
The cells still grow.

38
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.020
Give the drug again.

39
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.300
In this two-model cartoon, a drug acting only through that kinase shifts the curve

40
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.760
right, to higher doses.

41
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.880
One acting only through another target leaves it put.

42
00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.480
Mixed effects fall between.

43
00:03:00.583 --> 00:03:02.543
The measured curve barely moves.

44
00:03:04.043 --> 00:03:06.903
Cells without that kinase are stopped by about the same dose.

45
00:03:08.163 --> 00:03:11.623
So in these assays, the drug acts mainly through other targets.

46
00:03:12.717 --> 00:03:18.557
Now silence the protein's message, its m R N A, with R N A interference, and the job

47
00:03:18.557 --> 00:03:22.517
fails. But other messages may be silenced too.

48
00:03:23.757 --> 00:03:27.257
So put the protein back, in a form the interference cannot touch.

49
00:03:28.537 --> 00:03:30.817
How can it be the same protein, yet escape?

50
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.120
So: where does it live, in what order does it travel, does it cause the defect?

51
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.220
Before any result, ask what each model predicts.

52
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.020
Agreeing twins cannot choose.
